Dagu real file-access workload (observation only)
================================================

This standalone workload changes no kernel settings. It opens an existing
fixture read-only and verifies every byte through read, mmap, or both on one
file description. Fixture byte N must equal N modulo 256. Each invocation
opens a new file description. sequential/random apply POSIX_FADV_* to read;
they are not mmap advice coverage. Empty mmap fixtures are accepted without
issuing the invalid zero-length mmap syscall.

Build and verify the workload in the Linux container:

  cc -O2 -Wall -Wextra -Werror -static -o /tmp/dagu-ra-workload workload.c
  python3 test-workload.py /tmp/dagu-ra-workload

The test creates known fixtures at zero, 1, 4095, 4096, 4097 and 1048589 bytes,
checks all modes, then checks rejection of corrupt content. The host kernel
is not a substitute for Android execution. Native static AArch64 output can
also run on the Android target. On other hosts use an AArch64 static compiler.

Create a target fixture (container):

  python3 - <<'PY'
  from pathlib import Path
  Path('/tmp/dagu-ra-fixture').write_bytes(bytes(range(256)) * 4096)
  PY

Transfer and run (container, target serial must be confirmed first):

  timeout -k 2s 15s adb -s emulator-5554 push /tmp/dagu-ra-workload /data/local/tmp/dagu-phase1-workload
  timeout -k 2s 15s adb -s emulator-5554 push /tmp/dagu-ra-fixture /data/local/tmp/dagu-phase1-fixture
  timeout -k 2s 15s adb -s emulator-5554 shell 'chmod 755 /data/local/tmp/dagu-phase1-workload; /data/local/tmp/dagu-phase1-workload mixed /data/local/tmp/dagu-phase1-fixture'

On offline, inspect `adb devices`, reconnect this serial and retry at most
once. Persistent hangs require a bounded server restart and restoring the
original connection. Before repeating transfers or other mutations inspect
the destination and checksum; do not blindly repeat side effects. The
workload itself only reads the fixture and is safe to repeat.

These checks prove data/length correctness for these inputs only. They do
not prove a cold cache: freshly written files may remain resident. They do
not measure demand-versus-speculative IO, window limits, cgroup switching,
pressure transitions, concurrency or optimization benefit. Do not use their
PASS output to authorize a readahead policy. No global drop_caches is used.
The kernel's `filemap:filemap_readahead_window` trace event records the
candidate page range and async lookahead at the common page-cache submit
point. It covers read and mmap submit paths that reach this helper, but it
does not identify which API caused an event, separate demand pages from
speculation, or prove that every candidate page became storage I/O. Run
read and mmap workloads in separately controlled trials and correlate with
file/inode I/O evidence. This event alone does not satisfy the observation
contract or authorize a readahead policy; cgroup identity, pressure state,
demand-versus-speculative attribution and target-device validation remain
open. The target cost of enabling this event has not been measured.
